WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:07.200
Continue working on your project or open the file named 3-Assets, if you need to catch up.

2
00:00:08.019 --> 00:00:14.019
In addition to ready-made materials, there are other asset types that you can pull from the Material Library.

3
00:00:14.939 --> 00:00:17.940
Let's start by adding characters in the Theater area.

4
00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:25.190
The process is simple. You go to that category in the Assets Library and then choose a sub-section.

5
00:00:26.679 --> 00:00:32.179
You can work with posed humans, or better yet, animated characters among other types.

6
00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:38.969
But before you start dragging additional assets into the scene, it's best to plan the scene management ahead.

7
00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:44.469
You want to be able to control the visibility of certain elements independently from others.

8
00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:51.570
In this case, go to the scene graph and collapse the opened theater and context containers.

9
00:00:52.479 --> 00:00:56.979
Right-click the main scene graph entry and create a new container.

10
00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:00.219
Name it Characters.

11
00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:05.359
Next, right-click the newly created container and make it active.

12
00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:13.939
This means that from this point on, any physical asset you add to the scene would be part of that container, making it easier to control.

13
00:01:15.239 --> 00:01:21.239
Sure enough, add a character onto the stage. I will pick Camille in my case.

14
00:01:22.019 --> 00:01:27.019
Note how she automatically snaps onto the surface, ensuring she's well-grounded in place.

15
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:36.260
If you want to place her elsewhere, use the yellow center of the Transform gizmo to snap her to a different surface.

16
00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:45.370
Also note how she is part of the new active container in the scene graph.

17
00:01:46.319 --> 00:01:50.319
This means you can control her visibility without affecting the rest of the scene.

18
00:01:50.819 --> 00:02:03.230
You can make edits, of course, such as changing the color of her clothes or the kind of pose she is in.

19
00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.310
Since she's on stage, I'll have her perform a little dance.

20
00:02:09.969 --> 00:02:19.879
You can even pick a particular dance step from a few choices, and then fine-tune position and rotation if need be.

21
00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:29.610
Similarly, you can add more characters to act as an audience.

22
00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:37.449
It's just a question of choosing the right character and fine-tune the properties and the transforms to get them right.

23
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.080
Again, any new characters you add at this time will be part of the characters container.

24
00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:23.360
Aside from individual characters, you can also choose from groups of characters.

25
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.860
They are essentially individual animated characters that were grouped together for easier manipulation.

26
00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:40.659
The group itself can be repositioned but doesn't have any properties to edit.

27
00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:50.280
The individual characters underneath, on the other hand, have the same properties you have already learned to adjust.

28
00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:59.139
Similar to characters, you can add vehicles to the scene with a simple click and drag.

29
00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:06.090
First, make sure you create a new active container named Vehicles, much like you did earlier for characters.

30
00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:13.580
Go back to the root of the Assets library and choose the Vehicles subsection.

31
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.920
You have different types to choose from.

32
00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.480
For now, experiment with a bus and a car.

33
00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:34.569
Note that with cars, you can change the color after you place a car in the scene.

34
00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:45.480
The process is simple enough, but if you would like to animate characters walking about or vehicles driving past,

35
00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:50.110
then you can do that with Paths animation which we'll look at later.

36
00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:58.910
Other assets you may find useful are Decals, which you can use to add markings and dirt spots over other surfaces.

37
00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:02.540
They can be very useful for roads as you will see in a second.

38
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:09.420
Create a Decals active container. Consider that you can create a sub-container under a given section.

39
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:16.079
For example, you can create and activate a road decals container under Vehicles.

40
00:05:19.879 --> 00:05:21.879
Go to the Objects section of the library.

41
00:05:22.379 --> 00:05:29.560
Here, you'll find a collection of decals that you can use, including road markings.

42
00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:40.439
Keep in mind that you cannot use a conventional scale transform on decals, but you do have scale properties that you can adjust.

43
00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.980
You can select and duplicate decals as any other object using Shift-drag.

44
00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:53.490
Using the Instance option, choose parameter changes on one item applied to all others.

45
00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:07.170
Other types of useful decals allow you to add grunge or dirt onto surfaces.

46
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.800
Let's add a few stains on the asphalt.

47
00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:21.930
If you want these stains to affect the road markings, then you may need to adjust the sort order.

48
00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:36.480
As you can see, the library is quite extensive. Let's add some furniture to the cafe.

49
00:06:37.259 --> 00:06:47.579
Again, create a new active container for the occasion, call it Props, and ensure it's active.

50
00:06:49.139 --> 00:06:53.139
Go back one step to the Objects category and look into the Home section.

51
00:06:53.639 --> 00:06:57.970
Look into the Living Room, Tables section.

52
00:06:58.879 --> 00:07:01.379
Here we may find a few table types that we like.

53
00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:04.360
Drag one of them into the scene.

54
00:07:06.399 --> 00:07:13.899
You can add more of the same with additional drag and drop, or simply by duplicating the one you already inserted using Shift-drag.

55
00:07:14.399 --> 00:07:21.240
Take a moment to add a few more props, like plants and wall decorations.

56
00:07:32.639 --> 00:07:41.139
Again, if you mess up the positioning of an object, you can use the yellow center of the Transform gizmo to snap back the object onto any surface.

57
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:58.100
In addition to the native library, Twinmotion gives you full access to external libraries such as Quixel Megascans, Sketchfab, and even Adobe Substance 3D.

58
00:07:58.629 --> 00:08:01.629
These can all be found at the root of the Asset Library.

59
00:08:12.079 --> 00:08:19.529
Make sure you are signed in to your Epic account, and then you can download and place any asset from these external libraries.

60
00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:31.879
I think I'll add a couple of boulders at the entrance of the cafe. The process is quite straightforward.

61
00:08:36.769 --> 00:08:45.269
I still want to cover an important item, which is the User Library, which is one of my favorite features in Twinmotion and I'll do so in the next video.

62
00:08:45.769 --> 00:08:51.269
But first, I will show you an interesting tool that lets you replace selected objects with another asset.

63
00:08:51.269 --> 00:08:55.379
On the roof of the main building, you have several planters.

64
00:08:55.909 --> 00:09:01.409
They look quite good, but let's assume you want to replace them with another planter from the Asset Library.

65
00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:05.700
First, you select them in the viewport with the help of the Ctrl key.

66
00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:17.470
Then you right-click on one of them in the scene graph and choose Replace Object.

67
00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:26.210
Find the asset you want to use, in this case under Objects -> City -> Planters.

68
00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:30.210
Drag the buxom planter into the Replace Object panel.

69
00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:38.440
Make sure the Transform option is on, mostly to preserve rotations, and click the Start Replacement button.

70
00:09:39.169 --> 00:09:47.450
And there you have it. Save your work. In the next video, you'll learn about the User Library.
